Carla: Quick follow-up from the RFC thread — thinking about introducing 'per-layer mini-shard hydration lanes' for safetensors streaming. The idea: rather than a single hot/warm/cold axis, the loader exposes lightweight lanes (hot/high-priority, background/warm, bulk/cold) and the warmup coordinator maps requests to lanes based on prefix heat and deadline hints. This should let us hydrate tiny layers inline and progressively stream large layers without blocking critical tokens.

Ben: Nice. Can you clarify the ABI boundary? I want the loader to be manifest-agnostic (i.e., support manifestless blobs). We need a small, stable interface so runtime and loaders can evolve independently. Sketch: 
```
// pseudo-TS
interface WeightLoader {
  hydrate(layerId: string, lane: 'hot'|'warm'|'cold', deadlineMs?: number): Promise<{ token: string, ready: boolean }>;
  stream(layerId: string): AsyncIterable<Uint8Array>;
}
```
So hydrate returns a "snapshot token" the runtime can use for atomic swaps. If manifest exists, loader can optimize by returning chunk handles.

Maya: Concern: KV-cache coherence if lanes swap mid-response. If a request starts on a hot lane and we later demote the layer, how do we keep the cache valid? We can't let in-flight requests see mismatched versions.

Carla: Right — propose the hydrate call returns a short-lived "layer-snapshot" token (opaque string). The runtime pins that token for the duration of a request; any lane-based eviction will only apply to unpinned tokens. Migration between tokens requires atomic CAS semantics at the runtime layer.

Sasha: Also, some layers are tiny (e.g., bias vectors, small embedding segments). For those, sharding/streaming overhead is higher than copying inline. Suggest a configurable inline threshold (default 256KB). If layerSize <= threshold, loader should return ready=true synchronously and the stream can be a single in-memory buffer. :+1:

Ben: Telemetry hooks are necessary: per-lane hydrate latency, fallback counts (hot->warm->cold), and counts of inline vs streamed layers. ops-bot should create INF metrics for dashboards.

ops-bot: Created ticket INF-1234: "hydration-lanes-metrics" and assigned to #eng-ml.

Maya: We must avoid breaking the public weightsource API. Plan: add an internal adapter shim that implements the new lane semantics and translates to the existing `WeightSource.fetch` for older backends. Example fallback helper: 
```
async function hydrateWithManifestFallback(loader, layerId, lane){
  try {
    return await loader.hydrate(layerId, lane);
  } catch(e) {
    // fallback to index-first fetch
    const stream = await loader.stream(layerId);
    // drain small first-chunk to memory if it's tiny
    return { token: `fallback:${layerId}`, ready: true };
  }
}
```
This keeps the migration incremental.

Carla: Agreed. I'll draft a short RFC addendum covering: 1) loader API spec (hydrate/stream + token semantics), 2) inline-size threshold and config knobs, 3) runtime pinning/CAS behavior, 4) telemetry points. Goal: backwards-compatible to all existing weightsources.

Ben: One more tradeoff: if we expose lanes too liberally we risk blurring scheduling guarantees. We should start with 3 lanes (hot/warm/cold) and only expose lane hints (not strict routing) — runtime can disregard hints if capacity constrained.

Sasha: For sharding strategy, recommend lane-aware shard placement: put frequently-hot layers co-located to reduce cross-device jitter. We should add a placement hint in the token metadata.

Maya: Also add a graceful-degradation policy for partial streams: if stream fails mid-request, runtime should support fast fallback to a compact in-memory fetch path (best-effort) rather than failing the entire request.

Carla: Solid. I'll include a small example of migration-safe token CAS in the addendum. Planning to sync tomorrow 10:30am. If nobody objects, I'll open INF PR with the spec and tests.

Ben: No objections from me. :thumbsup:

Sasha: 👍

Maya: Sounds good.

Carla: Thanks — filing now.
